# The hypervisor nodes. /dev/gunyah is the kernel's Gunyah VM manager
# (vendor_gunyah_dev, a vm_manager_device_type like /dev/kvm). /dev/qgunyah
# is Qualcomm's own VM loader (vendor_gunyah_chr_device). Both labels are in
# vendor-volcano. Android's protected VMs use /dev/gunyah through crosvm, which
# the platform policy grants. No other domain gets a grant on either node.
#
# The platform keeps system and app domains off VM manager nodes, except
# crosvm. These rules extend that to vendor domains, and close Qualcomm's
# loader node to everyone. crosvm stays excluded because the platform grants
# it every VM manager node. Only userdebug builds ship crosvm for now.
neverallow { domain -crosvm_domain } vendor_gunyah_dev:chr_file { open read write append ioctl map };
neverallow domain vendor_gunyah_chr_device:chr_file { open read write append ioctl map };
